/-!
# The residue test on the small pro-etale site

Source: Weil conjectures blueprint, `sec-smooth-purity/sk-prop-sf-localization`,
node order 1867 (blueprint: lem-sf-proetale-residue-tor-test).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme

/-- Tor amplitude over `A = A_{E,n}` is detected by derived tensor with `A/(ϖ_E)`.
The quotient ideal is generated by the image of the recorded uniformizer in `A`;
`Ideal.Quotient.mk` is the canonical coefficient map. `K` is an arbitrary cochain
representative, and `f : P ⟶ K` is any K-flat resolution used by `derivedTensorRing`.

Source: Weil conjectures blueprint, localization unit, node order 1867
(blueprint: lem-sf-proetale-residue-tor-test).

Proof:
1. Apply def-weil-proetale-tor-amplitude to the constant residue module in degree zero.
2. For the converse use lem-pc-kflat-resolution to choose a K-flat resolution with
flat terms. By def-weil-derived-tensor, its tensor with a module computes derived tensor.
Write C for its tensor with the constant residue module; C need not be bounded.
3. For a residue-module sheaf N, tensor sectionwise over the constant presheaf field.
Vector-space short exact sequences split, so tensor preserves cycles and boundaries
and gives H^r(C(U) tensor N(U)) = H^r(C(U)) tensor N(U), canonically on classes of
pure tensors. These maps commute with restrictions independently of the splittings.
Use lem-pc-sites-module-sheafification-tensor and
lem-weil-pbc-site-module-presheaf-sheafification-exact to sheafify this identity.
The balanced maps (x tensor t) tensor y ↦ x tensor ty and
x tensor y ↦ (x tensor 1) tensor y identify C tensor N with P tensor N,
compatibly with restrictions and differentials (def-pur-sheaf-tensor-product).
Thus its cohomology vanishes outside the interval. No section ring of the
constant field sheaf is asserted to be a field.
4. Filter any A-module sheaf M by F_s M = image(ϖ_E^s : M ⟶ M).
We have F_0 M = M and F_n M = 0. Each successive quotient is killed by ϖ_E,
hence is a residue-module sheaf, to which step 3 applies. Flatness of each term
of P (def-pc-flat-sheaf-modules) makes tensor of each filtration sequence
short exact degreewise. Apply lem-weil-pbc-complex-ses-homology-long-exact
and descend from s = n to s = 0. Outside the interval the two adjacent terms
vanish, hence so does the middle term. Def-weil-derived-tensor identifies the
result with the derived tensor of K and M. Since M is arbitrary,
def-weil-proetale-tor-amplitude gives the required amplitude.
-/
theorem proEtale_torAmplitude_iff_residue_tensor
    (D : WeilConjectures.FiniteFieldDatum.{u})
    (l : ℕ) [Fact l.Prime] (_hl : l ≠ D.p)
    (E : Type u) [Field E] [Algebra (Padic l) E]
    [FiniteDimensional (Padic l) E] (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) (T : Scheme.{u})
    [∀ x : T, Invertible (l : T.residueField x)]
    (K P : CochainComplex (proEtaleRingModules T
      (proEtaleConstantRing T (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n))) ℤ)
    (f : P ⟶ K) (hf : QuasiIso f)
    (hP : CochainComplex.IsAcyclicTensorKFlat.{u + 1, u}
      (R := proEtaleConstantRing T (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n)) P)
    (_hK : intrinsicFiniteTorsionConstructible l E n hn T (DerivedCategory.Q.obj K))
    (a b : ℤ) (_hab : a ≤ b) :
    HasProEtaleTorAmplitude T (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n)
      (DerivedCategory.Q.obj K) a b ↔
      ∀ i : ℤ, (i < a ∨ b < i) →
        IsZero ((DerivedCategory.homologyFunctor (proEtaleRingModules T
          (proEtaleConstantRing T (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n))) i).obj
            (derivedTensorRing T (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n)
              (Padic.coefficientExtensionRing l E n ⧸ Ideal.span
                {Ideal.Quotient.mk (Padic.coefficientExtensionIdeal l E n)
                  (Padic.coefficientExtensionUniformizer l E)})
              (Ideal.Quotient.mk _) K P f hf hP)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
